Sure! Here's a simple HTML website that displays a random joke from a list of 4 jokes when a user clicks a button.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Random Joke Generator</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            text-align: center;
            margin-top: 50px;
            background-color: #f0f8ff;
        }
        #joke {
            font-size: 1.2em;
            margin-top: 20px;
            background-color: #e6f7ff;
            padding: 20px;
            border-radius: 10px;
            max-width: 600px;
            margin-left: auto;
            margin-right: auto;
        }
        button {
            padding: 10px 20px;
            font-size: 1em;
            cursor: pointer;
            background-color: #007BFF;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 5px;
        }
        button:hover {
            background-color: #0056b3;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>Random Joke Generator</h1>
    <button onclick="showRandomJoke()">Click for a Joke</button>
    <div id="joke"></div>

    <script>
        const jokes = [
            "Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything!",
            "What do you call fake spaghetti? An impasta!",
            "Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field!",
            "How do you organize a space party? You planet!"
        ];

        function showRandomJoke() {
            const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
            document.getElementById("joke").textContent = jokes[randomIndex];
        }
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works:
- The page has a button that, when clicked, calls the `showRandomJoke()` function.
- The function selects a random joke from the `jokes` array and displays it in the `#joke` div.

You can copy this code into a `.html` file and open it in a web browser to see it in action.

To change the color of the jokes to **red** using **CSS**, you can update the `#joke` selector in your CSS section. Here's how you can do it:

### ✅ Updated CSS (inside the `<style>` tag):

```css
#joke {
    font-size: 1.2em;
    margin-top: 20px;
    background-color: #e6f7ff;
    padding: 20px;
    border-radius: 10px;
    max-width: 600px;
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
    color: red; /* Add this line to change the text color to red */
}
```

### 🧠 Explanation:
- The `color: red;` property sets the text color of the content inside the `#joke` div to **red**.

### 📝 Result:
Now, whenever a joke is displayed, it will appear in **red** text on a light blue background.

### 🔄 Optional: If you want to change the background color too:
You can also update the `background-color` if you want a contrasting color with red text:

```css
#joke {
    background-color: #fff3f3; /* light pink */
    color: red;
}
